1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,997
Section seven's own scorecard put a p95 latency number on the table two lectures

2
00:00:04,997 --> 00:00:09,643
ago, right next to pass rate and cost, and moved on without explaining what that

3
00:00:09,643 --> 00:00:13,187
number actually means to the person waiting on the other end.

4
00:00:13,187 --> 00:00:15,975
Here's what it means, on its own: not very much.

5
00:00:15,975 --> 00:00:18,240
A user doesn't experience milliseconds.

6
00:00:18,240 --> 00:00:22,945
They experience whether the wait matched what the interaction led them to expect.

7
00:00:22,945 --> 00:00:27,824
Three point two seconds feels instant for a request that visibly involves real work.

8
00:00:27,824 --> 00:00:32,354
The same three point two seconds feels broken for something that looks like it

9
00:00:32,354 --> 00:00:33,516
should be immediate.

10
00:00:33,516 --> 00:00:38,395
That gap, between the measured number and the felt one, is where this lecture lives.

11
00:00:38,395 --> 00:00:42,867
Managing it is a real skill, separate from the modelling work you've done all

12
00:00:42,867 --> 00:00:47,688
section, and it's cheaper to fix than most teams assume, once you know which of the

13
00:00:47,688 --> 00:00:49,837
two problems you're actually solving.

14
00:00:50,387 --> 00:00:55,369
Set your budget from this table before you set it from a benchmark, because the same

15
00:00:55,369 --> 00:00:59,639
model call that's comfortably fast for a chat reply is unusably slow for

16
00:00:59,639 --> 00:01:04,443
autocomplete, and needlessly rushed for a report nobody's watching get generated.

17
00:01:04,443 --> 00:01:09,188
Inline autocomplete sits at the strict end for a specific reason: it's competing

18
00:01:09,188 --> 00:01:12,628
with the user's own typing speed, not with their patience.

19
00:01:12,628 --> 00:01:17,254
Miss that budget and the feature doesn't feel slow, it feels broken, because a

20
00:01:17,254 --> 00:01:21,998
suggestion that arrives after the word's already been typed manually isn't late,

21
00:01:21,998 --> 00:01:22,888
it's pointless.

22
00:01:22,888 --> 00:01:26,862
No amount of clever UI rescues a suggestion that missed its moment.

23
00:01:26,862 --> 00:01:31,606
A multi-step agent task can tolerate far more real time than a single chat reply

24
00:01:31,606 --> 00:01:35,283
can, but only if it narrates what it's doing while it does it.

25
00:01:35,283 --> 00:01:37,181
A silent minute reads as a hang.

26
00:01:37,181 --> 00:01:41,926
The exact same minute, spent visibly checking three things in sequence, reads as

27
00:01:41,926 --> 00:01:42,459
thorough.

28
00:01:42,459 --> 00:01:47,026
The batch row echoes last lecture's batching discount directly: when nobody's

29
00:01:47,026 --> 00:01:50,585
watching, the budget stops being a design constraint at all.

30
00:01:50,585 --> 00:01:54,558
Notice the table has no single "fast enough" number anywhere on it.

31
00:01:54,558 --> 00:01:55,626
That's deliberate.

32
00:01:55,626 --> 00:02:00,548
A budget copied from a different feature's interaction pattern is a guess wearing a

33
00:02:00,548 --> 00:02:05,411
benchmark's clothing, and it will be wrong in exactly the direction that costs you

34
00:02:05,411 --> 00:02:05,767
users.

35
00:02:06,317 --> 00:02:11,032
Walk through the refund agent from Section six one more time, because this is

36
00:02:11,032 --> 00:02:13,175
exactly where its latency shows up.

37
00:02:13,175 --> 00:02:18,196
Checking the account, checking the refund policy, issuing the approval: three tool

38
00:02:18,196 --> 00:02:22,911
calls in sequence, adding up to real seconds that no amount of model swapping

39
00:02:22,911 --> 00:02:27,748
removes, because the time is spent waiting on tools, not on the model thinking.

40
00:02:27,748 --> 00:02:32,585
A blank spinner for those seconds reads as a hang, and a user who's seen a hang

41
00:02:32,585 --> 00:02:35,218
before wonders whether to refresh the page.

42
00:02:35,218 --> 00:02:40,361
The identical wait, spent showing "checking your account, checking policy, approving

43
00:02:40,361 --> 00:02:45,382
refund" as each step completes, reads as a system doing visible, trustworthy work.

44
00:02:45,382 --> 00:02:47,892
Nothing about the actual latency changed.

45
00:02:47,892 --> 00:02:50,219
Everything about the felt latency did.

46
00:02:50,219 --> 00:02:54,995
That's not a trick, it's honest information delivered progressively instead of

47
00:02:54,995 --> 00:02:56,403
withheld until the end.

48
00:02:56,403 --> 00:03:01,485
The user was always going to wait those seconds; the only choice was whether they'd

49
00:03:01,485 --> 00:03:04,976
spend that wait informed or wondering if something broke.

50
00:03:04,976 --> 00:03:10,119
Model routing from two lectures ago earns a second mention here: it's one of the few

51
00:03:10,119 --> 00:03:12,752
techniques that moves both numbers at once.

52
00:03:12,752 --> 00:03:17,650
Send an easy step in that same three-call chain to a faster model and the actual

53
00:03:17,650 --> 00:03:19,854
wait shrinks, not just the felt one.

54
00:03:19,854 --> 00:03:24,814
Check this before reaching for narration alone: sometimes the honest fix is both,

55
00:03:24,814 --> 00:03:26,406
not a choice between them.

56
00:03:26,956 --> 00:03:31,580
Say the honest caveat plainly, because it's tempting to treat perceived-speed

57
00:03:31,580 --> 00:03:36,085
techniques as a substitute for the real fix rather than a complement to it.

58
00:03:36,085 --> 00:03:37,586
They aren't a substitute.

59
00:03:37,586 --> 00:03:42,631
An inline autocomplete suggestion that takes three seconds is broken, full stop, and

60
00:03:42,631 --> 00:03:47,135
no streaming animation turns three seconds into three hundred milliseconds.

61
00:03:47,135 --> 00:03:52,119
When the actual number blows through what the interaction pattern can tolerate, the

62
00:03:52,119 --> 00:03:56,624
fix is a faster model, a routing decision, less work happening per call, or

63
00:03:56,624 --> 00:04:01,428
sometimes admitting honestly that the interaction pattern was wrong for what the

64
00:04:01,428 --> 00:04:03,770
feature needs to do in the first place.

65
00:04:03,770 --> 00:04:06,113
So which situation are you actually in?

66
00:04:06,113 --> 00:04:11,037
Reach for perceived-speed fixes when the actual latency is close to budget and the

67
00:04:11,037 --> 00:04:14,761
remaining gap is about what happens on screen while it passes.

68
00:04:14,761 --> 00:04:19,745
Reach for the real fix, the harder one, when the actual number is nowhere close, no

69
00:04:19,745 --> 00:04:23,229
matter how good the waiting experience looks on top of it.

70
00:04:23,229 --> 00:04:28,093
Confusing the two is how teams ship a beautifully animated spinner over a feature

71
00:04:28,093 --> 00:04:29,835
that's still too slow to use.

72
00:04:30,385 --> 00:04:35,233
That's the term worth carrying forward, and the definition's order matters: set the

73
00:04:35,233 --> 00:04:40,081
budget from the interaction first, then check whether your architecture fits inside

74
00:04:40,081 --> 00:04:44,929
it, not the other way round, and not from whatever number the current build happens

75
00:04:44,929 --> 00:04:45,572
to produce.

76
00:04:45,572 --> 00:04:47,791
Run the measurement on something real.

77
00:04:47,791 --> 00:04:52,581
Most teams have only ever measured total completion time, the number that mattered

78
00:04:52,581 --> 00:04:57,254
for last lecture's scorecard, and have never separately measured how long a user

79
00:04:57,254 --> 00:04:59,882
waits before anything visible happens at all.

80
00:04:59,882 --> 00:05:04,555
That second number is usually the one actually shaping whether the feature feels

81
00:05:04,555 --> 00:05:09,287
fast, and it's often measured nowhere on the dashboards anyone actually looks at.

82
00:05:09,287 --> 00:05:14,076
If your feature streams already, check whether the first token arrives fast enough

83
00:05:14,076 --> 00:05:14,661
to matter.

84
00:05:14,661 --> 00:05:19,334
Streaming a reply that still takes two seconds to start doesn't buy you anything

85
00:05:19,334 --> 00:05:23,890
over a spinner, because the user spent the same two seconds staring at nothing

86
00:05:23,890 --> 00:05:24,532
either way.

87
00:05:24,532 --> 00:05:29,030
The saving only exists if the first visible sign of progress arrives close to

88
00:05:29,030 --> 00:05:33,878
instantly, and it's worth timing that specific number directly rather than assuming

89
00:05:33,878 --> 00:05:35,397
streaming alone solved it.

90
00:05:35,947 --> 00:05:37,748
One thing before you move on.

91
00:05:37,748 --> 00:05:39,673
Classify the interaction first.

92
00:05:39,673 --> 00:05:44,455
Autocomplete, chat, multi-step agent, or batch job, because the budget you're

93
00:05:44,455 --> 00:05:48,306
building toward depends entirely on which one you're building.

94
00:05:48,306 --> 00:05:52,342
Then look honestly at what a user currently sees during the wait.

95
00:05:52,342 --> 00:05:57,435
If the answer is nothing, a blank screen or a generic spinner, that's the cheapest

96
00:05:57,435 --> 00:06:01,348
fix available to you this section, cheaper than any model swap.

97
00:06:01,348 --> 00:06:06,192
Next lecture is the workshop: bring this section's cost model, model selection

98
00:06:06,192 --> 00:06:11,160
scorecard, and now a latency budget together into one unit economics model for a

99
00:06:11,160 --> 00:06:16,005
feature of your own, the artifact this whole section has been building toward.
